; 09a-thus.kurt

; `thus` is an alternative to `qed` (09-qed.kurt) for closing a `proof`
; block. Instead of re-deriving the shown goal from scratch (which `qed`
; does, searching the whole theory again), `thus` only checks that the
; block's *last line is already, literally, the goal* -- up to
; alpha-equivalence, the same notion of "the same formula" used elsewhere.

bool A, B

use A implies B
use A

show B
proof
    A
    B          ; the last line must spell out the goal exactly
thus

B              ; `B` is available outside the block, exactly like with `qed`

; because `thus` does no search at all, it's cheap and its outcome is easy
; to predict just by reading the last line -- but it also means it rejects
; a proof whose last line is one step short of the goal, even when `qed`
; would happily take that extra step. Try replacing the block above with:
;
;     show B "one step short"
;     proof
;         A
;     thus
;
; `qed` accepts this (it re-derives `B` from `A`); `thus` rejects it with a
; `ProofError`, since the last line (`A`) isn't literally the goal (`B`).

; prefer `thus` for long equational or `iff`-chain proofs, where every line
; already restates the goal-so-far (see `proofs/linear-algebra/group.kurt`
; for the style this is meant for) -- and `qed` for everything else.
